#use(std/code)

#section{Calls}::

A call is the only active form in YAMD: #code{#} followed by a name
(alphanumeric plus #code{-} and #code{/}). Headings, lists, links, tables and
math are all calls to library functions — never syntax.

The forms:

#list
	#item: #code{#name} — zero-argument call.
	#item: #code{#name(args)} — arguments are host (Racket) code.
	#item: #code|{#name(args){body}}| — plus an inline markup body.
	#item: #code{#name(args): rest} — body is the rest of the line.
	#item: #code{#name(args):} at end of line — body is the following indented block.
	#item: #code{#name(args)::} — body is the rest of the enclosing block.
	#item: #code{#name} alone on a line — sugar for #code{#name:}: body is the following indented block.

Bare call and keyword sugar — inside #code{(args)} an identifier ending in
#code{:} becomes a keyword argument (#code{style: "1"} reads as
#code{#:style "1"}); everything else is verbatim host code:

#code(lang: "plain")
	#hr

	#list(style: "1")
		#item: First
		#item: Second

	#image("cat.png" alt: "a napping cat")

Live:

#hr

#list(style: "1")
	#item: First
	#item: Second

#image("cat.png" alt: "a napping cat")

Corner cases:

#list
	#item: #code{(args)} and #code|{{body}}| are each optional and independent.
	#item: A call takes an inline #code|{{body}}| or a #code{: rest-of-line} body, never both — #code|{#name{a}: b}| does not parse as two bodies.
	#item: Because #code{(args)} is host code, a bare identifier there is a variable reference — write #code{#ref("vm")}, not #code{#ref(vm)}.
	#item: Separate multiple arguments with #strong{spaces}, not commas — #code{(args)} is host code, where #code{,} means unquote: #code{#toctree(numbered: #t depth: 2)}. (#code{#use} is the one form that also accepts commas.)
	#item: #code{#()} is an error (empty host escape, not a call).
